WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:02.550
Instructor: In this video, I want to use GitKraken

00:02.550 --> 00:04.950
as a way to visualize our branches

00:04.950 --> 00:08.220
and specifically to visualize the merging process.

00:08.220 --> 00:12.480
So I have GitKraken open with this playlist repository.

00:12.480 --> 00:14.086
We just merged our two branches,

00:14.086 --> 00:16.770
we merged oldies into master.

00:16.770 --> 00:20.850
Unfortunately, I've been trying desperately to zoom this in,

00:20.850 --> 00:23.430
this is as far as it lets me go for whatever reason,

00:23.430 --> 00:24.600
I hope that you can see this.

00:24.600 --> 00:27.180
Otherwise, I'll try and make it a bit larger

00:27.180 --> 00:30.030
in post-production, although it might be blurry.

00:30.030 --> 00:33.630
Anyway, I have my road trip playlist repository open,

00:33.630 --> 00:34.590
and if you need to know,

00:34.590 --> 00:36.150
if you don't remember how to open that,

00:36.150 --> 00:38.070
just go to view all repositories,

00:38.070 --> 00:40.980
or you can actually click right there to open a repository

00:40.980 --> 00:43.950
and then navigate to the right folder and open it.

00:43.950 --> 00:44.850
So here it is

00:44.850 --> 00:47.430
and I can see each branch referenced

00:47.430 --> 00:48.990
over here on the left side.

00:48.990 --> 00:51.300
Now, master and oldies are currently referring

00:51.300 --> 00:55.050
to the exact same commits because that's where they are,

00:55.050 --> 00:57.990
they are in sync with the one another right now.

00:57.990 --> 00:59.280
But I'm gonna change that,

00:59.280 --> 01:01.470
I'm actually going to add some more work

01:01.470 --> 01:03.120
to the oldies branch

01:03.120 --> 01:05.010
and then merge it back into master eventually.

01:05.010 --> 01:07.710
But for now, I'm gonna go to the oldies branch.

01:07.710 --> 01:12.360
So let's switch over, git switch oldies

01:12.360 --> 01:15.210
and then I'm just gonna add another song or two here.

01:15.210 --> 01:18.510
Okay, so I added two songs by Wings, "Band on the Run"

01:18.510 --> 01:19.710
and "Live and Let Die."

01:19.710 --> 01:21.300
Let's add both of those.

01:21.300 --> 01:26.300
Git commit -a -m, add 2 Wings songs.

01:28.740 --> 01:32.640
Okay, so we added that to oldies, master,

01:32.640 --> 01:35.850
no longer it's up to date or in sync with oldies.

01:35.850 --> 01:38.940
Oldies now has a commit that master does not.

01:38.940 --> 01:42.540
Obviously if I switch to master, we see that go away.

01:42.540 --> 01:44.970
So now if we go back to GitKraken,

01:44.970 --> 01:48.660
we can see here's master, the last commit master has

01:48.660 --> 01:51.840
is this one, add two Van Morrison songs.

01:51.840 --> 01:54.720
Oldies is now one commit ahead

01:54.720 --> 01:58.080
and it has add two Wings songs.

01:58.080 --> 02:01.860
So I can do one more example, let's just add two more songs.

02:01.860 --> 02:03.540
How about two Joni Mitchell songs?

02:03.540 --> 02:05.580
Lemme switch back, git switch,

02:05.580 --> 02:09.780
and I can switch branches using GitKraken or a GUI.

02:09.780 --> 02:12.390
So I can switch this way if you prefer.

02:12.390 --> 02:14.640
And now in my terminal I type git status,

02:14.640 --> 02:17.280
it does say I'm on oldies.

02:17.280 --> 02:20.250
So I prefer to use Terminal, but I like to use GitKraken

02:20.250 --> 02:22.050
to help visualize this stuff.

02:22.050 --> 02:25.980
Anyway, I'm going to add two Joni Mitchell songs.

02:25.980 --> 02:29.760
There we are, "River and "Both Sides Now," great songs.

02:29.760 --> 02:32.610
I will add those changes and commit.

02:32.610 --> 02:35.220
So git commit -a -m.

02:35.220 --> 02:37.043
And I could do this from within, GitKraken,

02:37.043 --> 02:38.403
if I wanted to.

02:39.840 --> 02:44.070
Over here, view the Change, I can stage that file,

02:44.070 --> 02:45.870
and then I can make my commit message

02:45.870 --> 02:50.550
add two Joni Mitchell songs.

02:50.550 --> 02:53.283
It's the exact same thing, whoops, I mean two,

02:54.150 --> 02:55.800
it's just I'm doing it from a GUI.

02:55.800 --> 02:57.060
So I stage that file,

02:57.060 --> 02:59.880
I commit and I'm on the branch, oldies.

02:59.880 --> 03:01.380
So let's do that.

03:01.380 --> 03:04.500
Okay, so now we see this is where master was,

03:04.500 --> 03:07.890
it still is, but oldies now has two new commits

03:07.890 --> 03:09.780
that master does not have.

03:09.780 --> 03:12.360
So this sets us up for what we've already seen,

03:12.360 --> 03:13.980
a fast forward merge.

03:13.980 --> 03:17.280
If I want to merge the changes from oldies into master,

03:17.280 --> 03:18.360
it's pretty straightforward,

03:18.360 --> 03:21.540
all we need to do is add these two commits to master.

03:21.540 --> 03:24.120
Well, it's not what we do, but it's what Git does,

03:24.120 --> 03:27.630
it just catches up, catches up to the top

03:27.630 --> 03:29.250
so it's in sync with oldies.

03:29.250 --> 03:32.910
So again, this is master right now, this is oldies.

03:32.910 --> 03:35.820
There's two commits on oldies that master does not have,

03:35.820 --> 03:37.350
I'm going to merge them.

03:37.350 --> 03:39.210
So what is that process like again?

03:39.210 --> 03:40.530
Well, it's two steps.

03:40.530 --> 03:43.050
Remember, we go to the destination branch,

03:43.050 --> 03:46.890
and then we merge with Git Merge and our branch name.

03:46.890 --> 03:48.603
So I'm gonna switch to master.

03:49.740 --> 03:54.420
Okay, now I'm gonna run, git merge, and then oldies.

03:54.420 --> 03:57.090
So before I hit Enter, just one more time,

03:57.090 --> 03:59.730
these two commits, Wings and Joni Mitchell,

03:59.730 --> 04:03.270
those two bands or two groups that I added songs for,

04:03.270 --> 04:06.390
those commits are now going to be added to master.

04:06.390 --> 04:08.400
Or another way of thinking of it is just master

04:08.400 --> 04:09.780
is going to fast forward

04:09.780 --> 04:11.400
and catch up with those two commits.

04:11.400 --> 04:14.430
So here we go, fast forward,

04:14.430 --> 04:16.530
and you can see over here now,

04:16.530 --> 04:20.910
master and oldies are both referring to the same commit.

04:20.910 --> 04:23.970
So that's visualizing a fast forward merge.

04:23.970 --> 04:26.933
But as I've alluded to, not every merge is a fast forward,

04:26.933 --> 04:29.640
and in the next video we will deliberately

04:29.640 --> 04:32.610
create a situation that is not as simple.

04:32.610 --> 04:35.490
We'll have to merge and there may be some issues that arise.

04:35.490 --> 04:36.490
So that's coming up.
